    {
      "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-amendment-data-retention-act-2015-sch-3-sec-186d",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "186D Ombudsman to be given information and access despite other",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-amendment-data-retention-act-2015#sch-3-sec-186d"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-amendment-data-retention-act-2015.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "telecommunications-interception-and-access-amendment-data-retention-act-2015.pdf, p.84",
      "values:originalText": "laws\n\n(1) Despite any other law, a person is not excused from giving\ninformation, answering a question, or giving access to a document,\nas and when required under this Chapter, on the ground that giving\nthe information, answering the question, or giving access to the\ndocument, as the case may be, would:\n\n(a) contravene a law; or\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2015A00039\n\nOversight by the Commonwealth Ombudsman  Schedule 3\nAmendments  Part 1\n\nNo. 39, 2015\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Amendment (Data\nRetention) Act 2015\n\n(b) be contrary to the public interest; or\n\n(c) might tend to incriminate the person or make the person\nliable to a penalty.\n\n(2) However:\n\n(a) the information, the answer, or the fact that the person has\ngiven access to the document, as the case may be; and\n\n(b) any information or thing (including a document) obtained as\na direct or indirect consequence of giving the information,\nanswering the question or giving access to the document;\nis not admissible in evidence against the person except in a\nproceeding by way of a prosecution for an offence against\nsection 133, 181A, 181B or 182, or against Part 7.4 or 7.7 of the\nCriminal Code.\n\n(3) Nothing in section 133, 181A, 181B or 182, or in any other law,\nprevents an officer of an enforcement agency from:\n\n(a) giving information to an inspecting officer (whether orally or\nin writing and whether or not in answer to a question); or\n\n(b) giving access to a record of the agency to an inspecting\nofficer;\nfor the purposes of an inspection under this Chapter of the\nagency’s records.\n\n(4) Nothing in section 133, 181A, 181B or 182, or in any other law,\nprevents an officer of an enforcement agency from making a record\nof information, or causing a record of information to be made, for\nthe purposes of giving the information to a person as permitted by\nsubsection (3).",
      "skos:definition": "laws\n\n(1) Despite any other law, a person is not excused from giving\ninformation, answering a question, or giving access to a document,\nas and when required under this Chapter, on the ground that giving\nthe information, answering the question, or giving access to the\ndocument, as the case may be, would:\n\n(a) contravene a law; or\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2015A00039\n\nOversight by the Commonwealth Ombudsman  Schedule 3\nAmendments  Part 1\n\nNo. 39, 2015\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Amendment (Data\nRetention) Act 2015\n\n(b) be contrary to the public interest; or\n\n(c) might tend to incriminate the person or make the person\nliable to a penalty.\n\n(2) However:\n\n(a) the information, the answer, or the fact that the person has\ngiven access to the document, as the case may be; and\n\n(b) any information or thing (including a document) obtained as\na direct or indirect consequence of giving the information,\nanswering the question or giving access to the document;\nis not admissible in evidence against the person except in a\nproceeding by way of a prosecution for an offence against\nsection 133, 181A, 181B or 182, or against Part 7.4 or 7.7 of the\nCriminal Code.\n\n(3) Nothing in section 133, 181A, 181B or 182, or in any other law,\nprevents an officer of an enforcement agency from:\n\n(a) giving information to an inspecting officer (whether orally or\nin writing and whether or not in answer to a question); or\n\n(b) giving access to a record of the agency to an inspecting\nofficer;\nfor the purposes of an inspection under this Chapter of the\nagency’s records.\n\n(4) Nothing in section 133, 181A, 181B or 182, or in any other law,\nprevents an officer of an enforcement agency from making a record\nof information, or causing a record of information to be made, for\nthe purposes of giving the information to a person as permitted by\nsubsection (3).",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-amendment-data-retention-act-2015-sch-3-sec-186d-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-amendment-data-retention-act-2015-sch-3-sec-186d-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-amendment-data-retention-act-2015-sch-3-sec-186d-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:telecommunications-interception-and-access-amendment-data-retention-act-2015-sch-3-sec-186d-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "8954b4ce200e1b10594592fbed1a8ba405c3e70d3a3ae2909815f7b5cdaf992c"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/telecommunications-interception-and-access-amendment-data-retention-act-2015#sch-3-sec-186d",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "186D Ombudsman to be given information and access despite other",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 84,
      "values:originalText": "laws\n\n(1) Despite any other law, a person is not excused from giving\ninformation, answering a question, or giving access to a document,\nas and when required under this Chapter, on the ground that giving\nthe information, answering the question, or giving access to the\ndocument, as the case may be, would:\n\n(a) contravene a law; or\nComLaw Authoritative Act C2015A00039\n\nOversight by the Commonwealth Ombudsman  Schedule 3\nAmendments  Part 1\n\nNo. 39, 2015\nTelecommunications (Interception and Access) Amendment (Data\nRetention) Act 2015\n\n(b) be contrary to the public interest; or\n\n(c) might tend to incriminate the person or make the person\nliable to a penalty.\n\n(2) However:\n\n(a) the information, the answer, or the fact that the person has\ngiven access to the document, as the case may be; and\n\n(b) any information or thing (including a document) obtained as\na direct or indirect consequence of giving the information,\nanswering the question or giving access to the document;\nis not admissible in evidence against the person except in a\nproceeding by way of a prosecution for an offence against\nsection 133, 181A, 181B or 182, or against Part 7.4 or 7.7 of the\nCriminal Code.\n\n(3) Nothing in section 133, 181A, 181B or 182, or in any other law,\nprevents an officer of an enforcement agency from:\n\n(a) giving information to an inspecting officer (whether orally or\nin writing and whether or not in answer to a question); or\n\n(b) giving access to a record of the agency to an inspecting\nofficer;\nfor the purposes of an inspection under this Chapter of the\nagency’s records.\n\n(4) Nothing in section 133, 181A, 181B or 182, or in any other law,\nprevents an officer of an enforcement agency from making a record\nof information, or causing a record of information to be made, for\nthe purposes of giving the information to a person as permitted by\nsubsection (3)."
    },
